## Likes Construction (μου αρέσει / αρέσουν)

<pattern name="likes-construction" scheme="neutral" source="verbs tags likes-singular, likes-plural">
<title>Likes Construction</title>
<subtitle>μου αρέσει / αρέσουν</subtitle>

<principle>
[Person] αρέσει (one thing) / αρέσουν (many things)
The thing liked is the SUBJECT!
</principle>

### One thing (αρέσει)
| Greek              | English           |
|--------------------|-------------------|
| Μου αρέσει         | I like (it)       |
| Σου αρέσει         | You like (it)     |
| Του/Της αρέσει     | He/She likes (it) |
| Μας αρέσει         | We like (it)      |
| Σας αρέσει         | You (pl.) like (it)|
| Τους αρέσει        | They like (it)    |

### Many things (αρέσουν)
| Greek              | English            |
|--------------------|--------------------|
| Μου αρέσουν        | I like (them)      |
| Σου αρέσουν        | You like (them)    |
| Του/Της αρέσουν    | He/She likes (them)|
| Μας αρέσουν        | We like (them)     |
| Σας αρέσουν        | You (pl.) like (them)|
| Τους αρέσουν       | They like (them)   |

</pattern>

---

## Quick Test: αρέσει or αρέσουν?

<decision-guide name="likes-test">
<intro>Choose the correct verb form based on what is liked.</intro>
<note>QuickTest colorScheme="ocean"</note>

### One thing is liked (singular subject) → αρέσει
| Greek                   | English               |
|-------------------------|-----------------------|
| Μου αρέσει ο καφές      | I like coffee         |
| Σου αρέσει η μουσική;   | Do you like the music?|

### Multiple things are liked (plural subject) → αρέσουν
| Greek                   | English               |
|-------------------------|-----------------------|
| Μου αρέσουν οι γάτες    | I like cats           |
| Τους αρέσουν τα βιβλία  | They like books       |

<summary>
Remember: the thing liked is the **subject**, so the verb agrees with it!
</summary>

</decision-guide>

---

## Name Construction (με λένε)

<pattern name="name-construction" scheme="neutral" source="phrases tag name-construction" render="only when the tag has rows">
<title>Name Construction</title>
<subtitle>με λένε = my name is (lit. "they call me")</subtitle>

<principle>
[Pronoun] λένε + name - literally "they call me..."
</principle>

| Greek              | English                    |
|--------------------|----------------------------|
| Με λένε...         | My name is... (they call me) |
| Σε λένε...         | Your name is...            |
| Τον/Την λένε...    | His/Her name is...         |
| Πώς σε λένε;       | What's your name?          |

</pattern>

---

## Quick Test: ίσως or μήπως?

<decision-guide name="hedging-test">
<intro>Both soften what you say, but they live in different sentences.</intro>
<note>QuickTest colorScheme="olive"; static copy in `tabs/patterns.tsx`</note>

### You are saying something — maybe, possibly → ίσως

| Greek             | English              |
|-------------------|----------------------|
| Ίσως πάρω ράμεν   | Maybe I'll have ramen|
| Ίσως αύριο        | Maybe tomorrow       |

### You are asking something — by any chance → μήπως

| Greek                        | English                          |
|------------------------------|----------------------------------|
| Μήπως έχετε τακογιάκι;       | Do you happen to have takoyaki?  |
| Μήπως ξέρεις τι ώρα είναι;   | Do you happen to know the time?  |

<summary>
ίσως never opens a question, μήπως never closes a statement. After ίσως the verb takes its short form with no θα: **Ίσως πάω**, not ίσως θα πάω.
</summary>

</decision-guide>

---

## Pattern Logic (background, not rendered)

<insight>
Both constructions flip the English perspective:

**"I like coffee"** → Literally: "To-me is-pleasing the-coffee"
- μου = to me (indirect object)
- αρέσει = is pleasing (verb agrees with coffee!)
- ο καφές = the coffee (subject)

**"My name is..."** → Literally: "They call me..."
- με = me (object)
- λένε = they call (3rd person plural)
- The "they" is impersonal

</insight>

